From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: BCM5301X: use IMP0 port 8 as the CPU port

The switch blocks of most BCM5301X boards in tree make port 5 (on some
boards 7) the CPU port and leave port 8, the IMP0 management port,
disabled. The switch sends frames it cannot place, such as a tagged
frame whose VID is missing from the VLAN table, only toward the
management port. With port 8 disabled those frames are dropped, so on a
standalone port a VLAN tagged uplink, for example a tagged PPPoE WAN,
never comes up.

Make port 8 the only CPU port on the 30 boards below and disable the
old CPU port, the layout bcm47094-netgear-r8500.dts already uses. Where
the old conduit stays enabled, it sits behind its disabled port and
carries no traffic.

gmac2 needs its own MAC cell. Without one bgmac takes et2macaddr from
nvram, which most of these CFEs do not set, and otherwise a random
address on every boot. gmac2 gets a cell on 27 boards:

- The MR26 and MR32 move their cell from gmac0 to gmac2.
- The EA6300 V1, RT-AC87U, the four Luxul boards and the K3 point gmac2
  at their existing et0macaddr or et1macaddr node, index 0. On the
  RT-AC3100, RT-AC3200, RT-AC5300 and RT-AC88U gmac2 already had a cell
  with index 2 (1 on the RT-AC88U); it drops to 0, which is also the
  address the vendor firmware gives gmac2.
- Twelve boards get a new brcm,nvram node with an et0macaddr cell,
  the address the old conduit read from nvram.
- The Archer C5 V2 and C9 V1 have no nvram partition. gmac2 reads the
  label MAC from their default-mac partition: a 32-bit length of 6,
  then the MAC at offset 8, the layout the vendor firmware reads.

The SR400ac and the R7900 get no cell, and bgmac reads et2macaddr from
nvram; on the Tenda AC9 port 8 is gmac0, which reads et0macaddr.

If a new node does not start on the nvram header, brcm_nvram fails to
probe and gmac2 waits for its cell forever, so the board has no
conduit. Each offset comes from that model where a read exists:

Board          Node                    Source
RT-N18U        NAND 0x80000 0x180000   partition table on the unit
RT-AC56U       NAND 0x80000 0x180000   OpenWrt boot log
RT-AC68U       NAND 0x80000 0x180000   OpenWrt boot log
R6250 V1       NAND 0x80000 0x180000   OpenWrt boot log
R6300 V2       NAND 0x80000 0x180000   OpenWrt boot log
R7000          NAND 0x80000 0x180000   OpenWrt boot log and /proc/mtd
R6400 V1       NAND 0x80000 0x180000   R6250 V1, R6300 V2, R7000
EA6500 V2      NAND 0x80000 0x100000   /proc/mtd of the EA6700
WZR-600DHP2    NAND 0xa0000 0x160000   CFE "show devices" on a unit
WZR-900DHP     NAND 0xa0000 0x160000   LEDE boot log
WZR-1750DHP    NOR  0xf0000 0x10000    dump of a unit, FLSH at 0xf0000
WXR-1900DHP    NOR  0x70000 0x10000    OpenWrt boot log

The EA6500 V2 has no log of its own. The EA6700 is the same board apart
from the LEDs, and its /proc/mtd matches the CFE layout in the Linksys
GPL. The R6400 V1 has no log of its own. The three Netgear NAND boards
above, on the same vendor firmware base, all keep nvram at that
offset. The two NOR nodes sit in the serial flash window, as the Luxul
nodes in tree do.

